Perfect 👍 this is exactly how interviewers want to hear it —
start naïve → find problems → evolve → final design.
I’ll walk you step-by-step like a real design discussion, from pure OOP + inheritance to the correct Strategy Pattern solution.

🦆 Duck Simulator – Design Journey (Beginner → Correct)
1️⃣ FIRST APPROACH: Only OOP + Inheritance
🧠 Thought process (Beginner thinking)

“All ducks can fly and quack, so let’s put everything in a base Duck class.”

📦 Initial Design (Naïve)
Class Diagram
           Duck
-------------------------
+ fly()
+ quack()
+ swim()
+ display()
-------------------------
      ▲
-------------------------
|         |            |
Mallard  RedHead   Rubber
Duck     Duck       Duck

Code
class Duck {
    void fly() {
        System.out.println("I can fly");
    }

    void quack() {
        System.out.println("Quack");
    }

    void swim() {
        System.out.println("All ducks swim");
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("I am a duck");
    }
}

❌ Problem Appears
RubberDuck

❌ Cannot fly

❌ Does not quack (it squeaks)

👉 Inheritance breaks

2️⃣ SECOND APPROACH: Method Overriding
🧠 Thought process

“Let RubberDuck override fly() and quack()”

Updated Class
class RubberDuck extends Duck {

    @Override
    void fly() {
        // do nothing
    }

    @Override
    void quack() {
        System.out.println("Squeak");
    }
}

❌ Why This Is Bad Design

🚨 Interview Red Flags

Code duplication (many ducks override same methods)

Violates SRP

Hard to maintain

Behavior fixed at compile time

Every new duck = more overrides

❓ What if tomorrow:

RocketDuck?

ToyDuck?

MutedDuck?

👉 Inheritance explosion

3️⃣ THIRD APPROACH: Interfaces (Still Wrong)
🧠 Thought process

“Let’s separate behaviors using interfaces”

Design
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

interface Quackable {
    void quack();
}

class MallardDuck extends Duck implements Flyable, Quackable { }
class RubberDuck extends Duck implements Quackable { }

❌ Still Not Correct
Problem	Explanation
❌ Code duplication	Every class implements fly/quack
❌ No reuse	Same fly logic repeated
❌ No runtime change	Behavior fixed
❌ Tight coupling	Duck tied to behavior

👉 Interfaces alone do not solve behavior variation

4️⃣ REAL PROBLEM IDENTIFIED 🔍
❓ What is actually changing?

✅ Behavior (fly, quack)

❓ What remains constant?

✅ Duck identity (Mallard, Rubber, Decoy)

👉 Key Design Principle

Encapsulate what varies

5️⃣ CORRECT SOLUTION: Strategy Pattern ✅
🧠 Thought process (Senior-level)

“Separate fly and quack into independent strategies and compose them.”

📐 Final Class Diagram (Correct)
UML (Final)
                Duck (abstract)
        --------------------------------
        - FlyBehavior flyBehavior
        - QuackBehavior quackBehavior
        --------------------------------
        + performFly()
        + performQuack()
        + swim()
        + display()  (abstract)
                    ▲
        --------------------------------
        |              |              |
   MallardDuck    RubberDuck      DecoyDuck


        FlyBehavior             QuackBehavior
        ------------            --------------
        + fly()                 + quack()
            ▲   ▲                    ▲   ▲
     FlyWithWings FlyNoWay       Quack   Squeak

6️⃣ Code (Correct Design)
Behavior Interfaces
interface FlyBehavior {
    void fly();
}

interface QuackBehavior {
    void quack();
}

Concrete Behaviors
class FlyWithWings implements FlyBehavior {
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Flying with wings");
    }
}

class FlyNoWay implements FlyBehavior {
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Cannot fly");
    }
}

Duck Class (Composition)
abstract class Duck {

    FlyBehavior flyBehavior;
    QuackBehavior quackBehavior;

    void performFly() {
        flyBehavior.fly();
    }

    void performQuack() {
        quackBehavior.quack();
    }

    void swim() {
        System.out.println("All ducks swim");
    }

    abstract void display();
}

Concrete Duck
class MallardDuck extends Duck {

    MallardDuck() {
        flyBehavior = new FlyWithWings();
        quackBehavior = new Quack();
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("I'm a Mallard Duck");
    }
}

7️⃣ BIG WIN: Runtime Behavior Change 🔥
Duck duck = new MallardDuck();
duck.performFly();   // Flying with wings

duck.flyBehavior = new FlyNoWay();
duck.performFly();   // Cannot fly


✅ Impossible with inheritance alone
✅ Huge design flexibility

🎯 FINAL INTERVIEW SUMMARY (Say This)

“Initially I used inheritance, but behavior variation caused duplication and rigidity.
Interfaces reduced inheritance but still duplicated behavior.
Finally, I identified that fly and quack vary independently and applied the Strategy Pattern using composition, which follows Open/Closed Principle and allows runtime behavior changes.”

🔑 Concepts You Demonstrated

OOP basics

Inheritance pitfalls

Composition over inheritance

Strategy Pattern

SOLID principles

Real-world design thinking